WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.127
<v Tutor>Good morning.

2
00:00:01.127 --> 00:00:06.386
<v Tutor>This is Part Two Daily for Saturday the seventh of November, and today is a spiral review.

3
00:00:06.386 --> 00:00:13.309
<v Tutor>Spiral reviews exist because memory decays on a curve, and the cheapest time to rescue a fact is just before you lose it.

4
00:00:13.309 --> 00:00:21.091
<v Tutor>So we go back to material from about one week ago and about three weeks ago, and we bring each topic back harder, tied to another system.

5
00:00:21.091 --> 00:00:23.130
<v Tutor>Today there are four revisits.

6
00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:30.751
<v Tutor>First, cardiogenic shock after a coronary intervention, with a kidney twist and the trial that took the balloon pump off its pedestal.

7
00:00:30.751 --> 00:00:38.747
<v Tutor>Second, the phosphate and calcium question from the 2025 first sitting, which then walks straight into citrate anticoagulation on the filter.

8
00:00:38.747 --> 00:00:44.114
<v Tutor>Third, TURP syndrome and the osmolar gap, with a twist where the measured osmolality is low.

9
00:00:44.114 --> 00:00:49.373
<v Tutor>Fourth, a four-part blood gas question run at exam pace, ending in metformin and dialysis.

10
00:00:49.373 --> 00:00:55.544
<v Tutor>There is no journal watch today, because nothing new and practice changing has appeared since the last one.

11
00:00:55.544 --> 00:01:04.560
<v Tutor>After the revisits, a syllabus round-up of six high-yield items from beyond this week: ventilation, airway, pulmonary embolism, poisoning, bleeding, and sepsis.

12
00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:05.902
<v Tutor>Have a pen ready.

13
00:01:05.902 --> 00:01:08.800
<v Tutor>There are pause and answer moments throughout.

14
00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:11.256
<v Tutor>Revisit one.

15
00:01:11.256 --> 00:01:12.629
<v Tutor>Picture the scene.

16
00:01:12.629 --> 00:01:14.371
<v Tutor>It is two in the morning.

17
00:01:14.371 --> 00:01:21.393
<v Tutor>A man in his late fifties comes up from the cath lab intubated, with a stent in his proximal left anterior descending artery.

18
00:01:21.393 --> 00:01:26.989
<v Tutor>His blood pressure is 82 over 50 on noradrenaline, his peripheries are cold, and the lactate is 6.

19
00:01:26.989 --> 00:01:29.470
<v Tutor>The nurse asks you what you want first.

20
00:01:29.470 --> 00:01:32.427
<v Tutor>That is almost word for word a College question.

21
00:01:32.427 --> 00:01:46.998
<v Tutor>From the 2023 first sitting, question one: Discuss the assessment and initial management, first hour, of an intubated patient admitted to the ICU with cardiogenic shock, after percutaneous coronary intervention for acute coronary syndrome due to a proximal LAD lesion.

22
00:01:46.998 --> 00:01:56.765
<v Tutor>Your answer should include but not be limited to the following headings: potential likely causes, suggested diagnostic approach, key elements of the management of likely causes.

23
00:01:56.765 --> 00:02:02.098
<v Tutor>The pass rate was 66.7 percent, and the College said it was a near repeat of a 2019 question.

24
00:02:02.098 --> 00:02:03.840
<v Tutor>So this theme comes back.

25
00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:06.864
<v Tutor>Here is the answer that earns the marks.

26
00:02:06.864 --> 00:02:13.917
<v Tutor>It is a long-answer question, so think in three headings, exactly as the stem gives them, and prioritise inside each one.

27
00:02:13.917 --> 00:02:21.079
<v Tutor>The examiners were explicit that generic shock answers, which wander into distributive and neurogenic shock, scored poorly.

28
00:02:21.079 --> 00:02:28.569
<v Tutor>They also said that mechanical support and emergency surgery should be ranked above minor measures like placing an arterial line.

29
00:02:28.569 --> 00:02:31.248
<v Tutor>First, the likely causes in this patient.

30
00:02:31.248 --> 00:02:34.857
<v Tutor>Pump failure from a large anterior infarct is the default.

31
00:02:34.857 --> 00:02:39.559
<v Tutor>But a proximal LAD lesion that has just been stented can still be going wrong.

32
00:02:39.559 --> 00:02:43.167
<v Tutor>Think stent thrombosis, coronary dissection, or no-reflow.

33
00:02:43.167 --> 00:02:52.352
<v Tutor>Then the mechanical complications: a ventricular septal rupture, acute mitral regurgitation from papillary muscle rupture, and free wall rupture with tamponade.

34
00:02:52.352 --> 00:03:03.068
<v Tutor>Add an arrhythmia, bleeding from the access site, including a retroperitoneal haematoma after a femoral puncture, and the haemodynamic effect of sedation and positive pressure ventilation.

35
00:03:03.068 --> 00:03:04.982
<v Tutor>And one less obvious cause.

36
00:03:04.982 --> 00:03:14.440
<v Tutor>An apical anterior infarct treated with inotropes can develop dynamic left ventricular outflow tract obstruction, which gets worse with every increase in adrenaline.

37
00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:16.861
<v Tutor>Second, the diagnostic approach.

38
00:03:16.861 --> 00:03:22.577
<v Tutor>Echocardiography is the single most valuable test, and it should be done in the first minutes.

39
00:03:22.577 --> 00:03:26.612
<v Tutor>It tells you the ejection fraction and the regional wall motion.

40
00:03:26.612 --> 00:03:34.009
<v Tutor>It shows a septal defect with colour flow, a flail mitral leaflet, a pericardial collection, and right ventricular function.

41
00:03:34.009 --> 00:03:38.772
<v Tutor>Then a twelve-lead ECG for new ST elevation, which suggests stent thrombosis.

42
00:03:38.772 --> 00:03:45.216
<v Tutor>Then a gas with lactate, a haemoglobin to look for bleeding, potassium and magnesium, and a troponin trend.

43
00:03:45.216 --> 00:03:49.811
<v Tutor>Talk to the interventional cardiologist early about going back to the lab.

44
00:03:49.811 --> 00:04:00.906
<v Tutor>A pulmonary artery catheter is reasonable in refractory shock, because it measures cardiac output, wedge pressure and mixed venous saturation, and it lets you calculate cardiac power output.

45
00:04:00.906 --> 00:04:03.540
<v Tutor>Third, management of the likely causes.

46
00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:10.768
<v Tutor>Use a mean arterial pressure target of about 65 millimetres of mercury, with noradrenaline as the first line vasopressor.

47
00:04:10.768 --> 00:04:16.540
<v Tutor>Add an inodilator, dobutamine or milrinone, for a low cardiac output once pressure is restored.

48
00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:27.710
<v Tutor>Positive end expiratory pressure helps the failing left ventricle, because it lowers both preload and afterload, and the College specifically marked down candidates who could not explain that.

49
00:04:27.710 --> 00:04:31.831
<v Tutor>Each mechanical complication goes to the surgeon, usually urgently.

50
00:04:31.831 --> 00:04:38.865
<v Tutor>For refractory shock, escalate to mechanical support, a microaxial flow pump or venoarterial ECMO, through a shock team.

51
00:04:38.865 --> 00:04:45.349
<v Tutor>Grade the severity with the five SCAI stages, A to E, which makes your answer look like a consultant wrote it.

52
00:04:45.349 --> 00:04:47.822
<v Tutor>The stages are worth knowing by name.

53
00:04:47.822 --> 00:04:51.559
<v Tutor>A is at risk, such as a large infarct without hypoperfusion.

54
00:04:51.559 --> 00:04:56.284
<v Tutor>B is beginning shock, with hypotension or tachycardia but preserved perfusion.

55
00:04:56.284 --> 00:05:01.505
<v Tutor>C is classic shock, with hypoperfusion needing a vasoactive drug or mechanical support.

56
00:05:01.505 --> 00:05:05.351
<v Tutor>D is deteriorating, meaning worsening despite initial therapy.

57
00:05:05.351 --> 00:05:10.407
<v Tutor>E is extremis, including refractory arrest or ongoing cardiopulmonary resuscitation.

58
00:05:10.407 --> 00:05:16.231
<v Tutor>Our man, intubated on noradrenaline with a lactate of 6 and cold peripheries, is at least stage C.

59
00:05:16.231 --> 00:05:23.320
<v Tutor>If he needs a second vasoactive drug within the hour, he is stage D, and that is when the shock team decides on a device.

60
00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:29.060
<v Tutor>Stage it out loud in your answer, because it shows the examiner a rationale for escalation rather than a list.

61
00:05:29.960 --> 00:05:31.523
<v Examiner>I would add one thing.

62
00:05:31.523 --> 00:05:34.597
<v Examiner>Candidates write mechanical support and stop there.

63
00:05:34.597 --> 00:05:38.452
<v Examiner>Which device, for what problem, and what does it cost the patient?

64
00:05:38.452 --> 00:05:41.994
<v Examiner>A balloon pump does not rescue a heart that cannot contract.

65
00:05:41.994 --> 00:05:45.016
<v Examiner>ECMO raises afterload on a stunned left ventricle.

66
00:05:45.016 --> 00:05:47.360
<v Examiner>Say that and you are in the top half.

67
00:05:48.260 --> 00:05:52.584
<v Tutor>That brings us to the trial that changed the balloon pump story.

68
00:05:52.584 --> 00:05:57.629
<v Tutor>IABP-SHOCK II, Thiele and colleagues, New England Journal of Medicine, 2012.

69
00:05:57.629 --> 00:06:06.697
<v Tutor>Six hundred patients at 37 German centres with myocardial infarction complicated by cardiogenic shock, all planned for early revascularisation.

70
00:06:06.697 --> 00:06:11.922
<v Tutor>They were randomised to intra-aortic balloon counterpulsation or to no balloon.

71
00:06:11.922 --> 00:06:19.969
<v Tutor>The primary outcome was 30-day all-cause mortality, 39.7 versus 41.3 percent, a relative risk of 0.96, with a P value of 0.69.

72
00:06:19.969 --> 00:06:25.014
<v Tutor>There was no difference in bleeding, peripheral ischaemia, sepsis or stroke.

73
00:06:25.014 --> 00:06:29.938
<v Tutor>Follow-up at one year and at six years showed the same absence of benefit.

74
00:06:29.938 --> 00:06:40.268
<v Tutor>The main criticisms were that it was unblinded, about one in ten control patients crossed over to a balloon, and the control group had a higher lactate at baseline.

75
00:06:40.268 --> 00:06:53.900
<v Tutor>What it changed: the European Society of Cardiology now recommends against routine balloon pump use in cardiogenic shock, reserving it for mechanical complications such as a septal rupture or acute mitral regurgitation.

76
00:06:54.080 --> 00:07:09.240
<v Tutor>One sentence for an SAQ: in IABP-SHOCK II, routine balloon pump support for infarct-related cardiogenic shock did not reduce 30-day mortality, so it is not used routinely, though it can still bridge a mechanical complication to surgery.

77
00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:12.942
<v Tutor>Now the difficulty goes up, and the kidney joins in.

78
00:07:12.942 --> 00:07:14.152
<v Tutor>It is day two.

79
00:07:14.152 --> 00:07:19.380
<v Tutor>The lactate has cleared to 1.8 and he is on a low dose of noradrenaline and dobutamine.

80
00:07:19.380 --> 00:07:22.957
<v Tutor>But he has passed 80 millilitres of urine in eight hours.

81
00:07:22.957 --> 00:07:26.699
<v Tutor>The creatinine has gone from 90 to 210 micromoles per litre.

82
00:07:26.699 --> 00:07:32.312
<v Tutor>His central venous pressure is 18 millimetres of mercury and his mean arterial pressure is 68.

83
00:07:32.312 --> 00:07:35.999
<v Tutor>The registrar wants to give a fluid bolus for the oliguria.

84
00:07:35.999 --> 00:07:37.320
<v Tutor>What do you say?

85
00:07:38.220 --> 00:07:39.919
<v Candidate>I would not give fluid.

86
00:07:39.919 --> 00:07:43.481
<v Candidate>His output is improving and his filling pressure is high.

87
00:07:43.481 --> 00:07:46.166
<v Candidate>I think the problem is venous congestion.

88
00:07:46.166 --> 00:07:54.057
<v Candidate>The perfusion pressure of the kidney is roughly the mean arterial pressure minus the central venous pressure, so 68 minus 18 is only 50.

89
00:07:54.057 --> 00:08:00.250
<v Candidate>I would try a loop diuretic, perhaps frusemide 40 to 80 milligrams intravenously, and check the response.

90
00:08:00.250 --> 00:08:05.620
<v Candidate>If he does not respond, I would talk to the nephrologists about renal replacement therapy.

91
00:08:06.120 --> 00:08:06.750
<v Examiner>Good.

92
00:08:06.750 --> 00:08:10.383
<v Examiner>That is cardiorenal syndrome type one, and you have the physiology.

93
00:08:10.383 --> 00:08:12.370
<v Examiner>But you have left out two things.

94
00:08:12.370 --> 00:08:15.761
<v Examiner>What would make you start dialysis today rather than tomorrow?

95
00:08:15.761 --> 00:08:17.360
<v Examiner>And what about the drugs?

96
00:08:17.860 --> 00:08:26.024
<v Candidate>The urgent indications are refractory hyperkalaemia, severe acidosis, pulmonary oedema that is not responding to diuretics, and uraemic complications.

97
00:08:26.024 --> 00:08:31.760
<v Candidate>Without those, the trials of earlier versus delayed starting do not show a benefit from starting early.

98
00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:38.064
<v Candidate>For the drugs, I would review anything renally cleared, such as low molecular weight heparin and some antibiotics.

99
00:08:38.064 --> 00:08:41.940
<v Candidate>I would also stop nephrotoxins and avoid more contrast if possible.

100
00:08:42.840 --> 00:08:45.431
<v Tutor>That is the answer that earns the marks.

101
00:08:45.431 --> 00:08:49.480
<v Tutor>Notice how the higher-level move is to say congestion, not dryness.

102
00:08:49.480 --> 00:08:55.851
<v Tutor>A rising creatinine with a high central venous pressure in a failing heart is a reason to unload, not to fill.

103
00:08:55.851 --> 00:08:57.794
<v Tutor>Time for a pause and answer.

104
00:08:57.794 --> 00:09:04.596
<v Tutor>Name the three mechanical complications of myocardial infarction and the single echo finding that identifies each one.

105
00:09:04.596 --> 00:09:06.000
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

106
00:09:11.400 --> 00:09:20.601
<v Tutor>Ventricular septal rupture shows a left-to-right jet across the septum on colour Doppler, with a step-up in oxygen saturation from the right atrium to the pulmonary artery.

107
00:09:20.601 --> 00:09:26.020
<v Tutor>Papillary muscle rupture shows a flail mitral leaflet with severe, often eccentric, regurgitation.

108
00:09:26.020 --> 00:09:32.205
<v Tutor>And free wall rupture shows a pericardial effusion with tamponade physiology, often with clot in the pericardium.

109
00:09:32.205 --> 00:09:35.835
<v Tutor>All three typically present days, not hours, after the infarct.

110
00:09:35.835 --> 00:09:39.720
<v Tutor>All three need a surgeon, and a balloon pump is a reasonable bridge.

111
00:09:40.120 --> 00:09:41.336
<v Tutor>Revisit two.

112
00:09:41.336 --> 00:09:45.228
<v Tutor>This one comes from the 2025 first sitting, question 21.

113
00:09:45.228 --> 00:09:46.748
<v Tutor>It has two parts.

114
00:09:46.748 --> 00:09:54.835
<v Tutor>Part one gives you an elderly patient admitted with falls, who then develops hypoxia and heart failure despite a normal echo.

115
00:09:54.835 --> 00:10:06.936
<v Tutor>The phosphate is less than 0.10 millimoles per litre, the potassium is 3.1, the magnesium is 0.65, the albumin is 14 grams per litre, and the urea, creatinine and creatine kinase are all low.

116
00:10:06.936 --> 00:10:19.523
<v Tutor>Part two reads like this: A patient recently discharged from hospital following a long admission for management of variceal bleeding and decompensated alcoholic cardiomyopathy is referred to the ICU.

117
00:10:19.523 --> 00:10:25.421
<v Tutor>They have presented to the emergency department with refractory seizures and hypotension.

118
00:10:25.421 --> 00:10:37.278
<v Tutor>The key values are an ionised calcium of 0.61 millimoles per litre, a creatinine of 145 micromoles per litre, a urea of 9.4, a potassium of 5.1, a bicarbonate of 20, and an albumin of 19.

119
00:10:37.278 --> 00:10:42.143
<v Tutor>Part a asks for five possible causes of these biochemical abnormalities.

120
00:10:42.143 --> 00:10:47.372
<v Tutor>Part b asks for five investigations that would help discriminate between them.

121
00:10:47.372 --> 00:10:54.000
<v Tutor>You scored three and a half on this on the 14th of October and two and a half on the 17th of October.

122
00:10:54.180 --> 00:10:58.036
<v Tutor>The pass rate was 57.1 percent, and the highest mark in the room was 5.

123
00:10:58.036 --> 00:11:00.330
<v Tutor>So it was a hard question for everyone.

124
00:11:00.330 --> 00:11:03.844
<v Tutor>But the examiner comments tell you exactly where the marks were.

125
00:11:03.844 --> 00:11:08.042
<v Tutor>Many candidates failed to identify severe hypocalcaemia and renal dysfunction.

126
00:11:08.042 --> 00:11:13.020
<v Tutor>And answers on the causes of seizures that were not linked to the biochemistry scored nothing.

127
00:11:13.420 --> 00:11:15.047
<v Tutor>So let us rebuild it.

128
00:11:15.047 --> 00:11:20.097
<v Tutor>In part one, the significance of a phosphate below 0.1 is cellular energy failure.

129
00:11:20.097 --> 00:11:24.136
<v Tutor>Without phosphate you cannot make ATP or 2,3-diphosphoglycerate.

130
00:11:24.136 --> 00:11:34.684
<v Tutor>That gives respiratory muscle weakness, which explains the hypoxia and tachypnoea, and impaired myocardial contractility, which explains heart failure despite a recent normal echo.

131
00:11:34.684 --> 00:11:40.014
<v Tutor>The low urea, creatinine and creatine kinase point to malnutrition and low muscle mass.

132
00:11:40.014 --> 00:11:47.644
<v Tutor>The low potassium and magnesium fit diuretics and refeeding, and the potassium will not correct until the magnesium is replaced.

133
00:11:47.644 --> 00:11:50.955
<v Tutor>One fix from your answers is worth making explicit.

134
00:11:50.955 --> 00:11:55.499
<v Tutor>Check each value against its own reference range before you interpret it.

135
00:11:55.499 --> 00:11:59.595
<v Tutor>The ionised calcium of 1.31 is normal in this question, not high.

136
00:11:59.595 --> 00:12:03.466
<v Tutor>And Addison's disease causes a high potassium, not a low one.

137
00:12:03.466 --> 00:12:06.384
<v Tutor>Five likely causes of the hypophosphataemia.

138
00:12:06.384 --> 00:12:11.882
<v Tutor>Refeeding syndrome, which is an intracellular shift driven by insulin once feeding starts.

139
00:12:11.882 --> 00:12:16.202
<v Tutor>Renal phosphate loss from diuretics, alcohol, or hyperparathyroidism.

140
00:12:16.202 --> 00:12:21.981
<v Tutor>Parathyroid hormone related peptide from an occult malignancy, given the falls and the decline.

141
00:12:21.981 --> 00:12:25.740
<v Tutor>Poor intake and absorption, including vitamin D deficiency.

142
00:12:25.920 --> 00:12:33.997
<v Tutor>And intravenous iron, specifically ferric carboxymaltose, which raises fibroblast growth factor 23 and causes phosphate wasting.

143
00:12:33.997 --> 00:12:35.956
<v Tutor>Five slots, five answers.

144
00:12:35.956 --> 00:12:37.797
<v Tutor>Do not leave one blank.

145
00:12:37.797 --> 00:12:44.627
<v Tutor>Because refeeding sits at the top of that list, know how to prevent it, as it is examined in its own right.

146
00:12:44.627 --> 00:12:59.949
<v Tutor>The NICE criteria for high risk include a body mass index below 16, unintentional weight loss of more than 15 percent in three to six months, little or no intake for more than ten days, and low potassium, phosphate or magnesium before feeding starts.

147
00:12:59.949 --> 00:13:06.897
<v Tutor>Having two lesser criteria, such as a body mass index below 18.5 or a history of alcohol misuse, also counts.

148
00:13:06.897 --> 00:13:14.320
<v Tutor>For a high-risk patient, start at no more than 10 kilocalories per kilogram per day, and 5 in the most extreme cases.

149
00:13:14.320 --> 00:13:19.903
<v Tutor>Give thiamine and a B vitamin preparation before and during the first days of feeding.

150
00:13:19.903 --> 00:13:27.088
<v Tutor>Then check phosphate, potassium and magnesium daily, replace them, and build up the feed over four to seven days.

151
00:13:27.088 --> 00:13:34.630
<v Tutor>In the ICU, a fall in phosphate after feeding begins is the trigger to slow the feed, not to stop nutrition altogether.

152
00:13:34.630 --> 00:13:35.640
<v Tutor>Part two.

153
00:13:35.820 --> 00:13:47.756
<v Tutor>Name the problem first: severe hypocalcaemia, ionised 0.61, with acute kidney injury, in a patient with alcohol-related liver and heart disease, presenting with seizures and hypotension.

154
00:13:47.756 --> 00:13:52.370
<v Tutor>Both the seizures and the hypotension are explained by the calcium.

155
00:13:52.370 --> 00:13:53.601
<v Tutor>Five causes.

156
00:13:53.601 --> 00:14:02.461
<v Tutor>First, hypomagnesaemia, from alcohol and proton pump inhibitors, which impairs both the secretion and the action of parathyroid hormone.

157
00:14:02.461 --> 00:14:06.891
<v Tutor>Second, acute pancreatitis, with calcium being deposited in fat.

158
00:14:06.891 --> 00:14:12.982
<v Tutor>Third, vitamin D deficiency, from malnutrition and impaired hydroxylation in liver disease.

159
00:14:12.982 --> 00:14:16.366
<v Tutor>Fourth, renal failure with phosphate retention.

160
00:14:16.366 --> 00:14:22.149
<v Tutor>Fifth, rhabdomyolysis from the seizures, where calcium is deposited in injured muscle.

161
00:14:22.149 --> 00:14:30.086
<v Tutor>The College accepted vitamin D, parathyroid hormone and its related peptide, and amylase or lipase as the investigations.

162
00:14:30.086 --> 00:14:36.300
<v Tutor>Add magnesium, phosphate, creatine kinase and lactate, and an ECG to look at the QT interval.

163
00:14:37.200 --> 00:14:38.315
<v Examiner>And treatment?

164
00:14:38.315 --> 00:14:40.949
<v Examiner>The question did not ask for it, but I will.

165
00:14:40.949 --> 00:14:42.520
<v Examiner>The patient is seizing.

166
00:14:43.020 --> 00:14:51.475
<v Candidate>Ten to twenty millilitres of 10 percent calcium gluconate over ten minutes, which is about 2.2 millimoles of calcium for each ten millilitres.

167
00:14:51.475 --> 00:14:57.787
<v Candidate>Or ten millilitres of 10 percent calcium chloride through a central line, which is about 6.8 millimoles.

168
00:14:57.787 --> 00:15:01.789
<v Candidate>Then an infusion, with ionised calcium checked every few hours.

169
00:15:01.789 --> 00:15:06.580
<v Candidate>And I would replace magnesium at the same time, or the calcium will not hold.

170
00:15:07.080 --> 00:15:07.803
<v Examiner>Correct.

171
00:15:07.803 --> 00:15:10.966
<v Examiner>Calcium chloride gives three times the calcium per millilitre.

172
00:15:10.966 --> 00:15:13.000
<v Examiner>That is a number an examiner can ask.

173
00:15:13.900 --> 00:15:17.780
<v Tutor>Now raise the difficulty and bring in this week's renal block.

174
00:15:17.780 --> 00:15:20.163
<v Tutor>Same principles, different patient.

175
00:15:20.163 --> 00:15:28.421
<v Tutor>A woman with alcohol-related cirrhosis and septic shock is on continuous venovenous haemodiafiltration with regional citrate anticoagulation.

176
00:15:28.421 --> 00:15:33.742
<v Tutor>Over 24 hours her systemic ionised calcium falls from 1.05 to 0.85 millimoles per litre.

177
00:15:33.742 --> 00:15:36.402
<v Tutor>Her total calcium rises from 2.2 to 2.9.

178
00:15:36.402 --> 00:15:39.173
<v Tutor>Her anion gap opens, and her lactate is 5.

179
00:15:39.173 --> 00:15:40.559
<v Tutor>What is going on?

180
00:15:40.559 --> 00:15:43.441
<v Tutor>It helps to know where the filter came from.

181
00:15:43.441 --> 00:15:53.916
<v Tutor>In the Netherlands during the German occupation, a young physician named Willem Kolff, working in the small hospital at Kampen, built an artificial kidney out of what he could find.

182
00:15:53.916 --> 00:16:02.950
<v Tutor>He used sausage casing made of cellophane as the membrane, juice cans, and parts from an old washing machine, with the tubing wound around a rotating drum.

183
00:16:02.950 --> 00:16:10.598
<v Tutor>The cellophane let small molecules like urea pass from the blood into a bath of fluid by diffusion, down a concentration gradient.

184
00:16:10.598 --> 00:16:14.145
<v Tutor>His first fifteen patients with kidney failure all died.

185
00:16:14.145 --> 00:16:19.300
<v Tutor>The sixteenth, in 1945, was a comatose woman in kidney failure named Maria Schafstad.

186
00:16:19.300 --> 00:16:23.900
<v Tutor>She was a known Nazi collaborator, held as a prisoner after the liberation.

187
00:16:24.080 --> 00:16:28.999
<v Tutor>Kolff treated her anyway, and she was the first person to survive because of dialysis.

188
00:16:28.999 --> 00:16:39.674
<v Tutor>Kolff gave several of his early machines to hospitals abroad, later moved to the United States, and went on to work on artificial organs for the rest of his career, including the artificial heart.

189
00:16:39.674 --> 00:16:48.047
<v Tutor>Kolff's machine needed anticoagulation to keep blood from clotting on the membrane, and the problem he faced is still the problem we solve with citrate.

190
00:16:48.047 --> 00:16:50.977
<v Tutor>Two exam points come straight out of that story.

191
00:16:50.977 --> 00:16:53.489
<v Tutor>First, his drum was a diffusion machine.

192
00:16:53.489 --> 00:16:55.059
<v Tutor>Diffusion is dialysis.

193
00:16:55.059 --> 00:17:00.397
<v Tutor>Convection, where water is pushed across the membrane and drags solute with it, is filtration.

194
00:17:00.397 --> 00:17:09.502
<v Tutor>Today's question on continuous venovenous haemofiltration asks about pure convection, and the College noted that many candidates described haemodiafiltration instead.

195
00:17:09.502 --> 00:17:16.200
<v Tutor>Second, the circuit has to stay unclotted without making the patient bleed, which is exactly what regional citrate does.

196
00:17:16.600 --> 00:17:21.838
<v Tutor>Citrate is infused before the filter and chelates ionised calcium in the circuit.

197
00:17:21.838 --> 00:17:29.254
<v Tutor>The target post-filter ionised calcium is roughly 0.25 to 0.35 millimoles per litre, which stops the clotting cascade.

198
00:17:29.254 --> 00:17:33.551
<v Tutor>Some of the calcium citrate complex is removed across the filter.

199
00:17:33.551 --> 00:17:41.673
<v Tutor>The rest returns to the patient, where the liver, and to a lesser extent muscle and kidney, metabolise citrate in the Krebs cycle.

200
00:17:41.673 --> 00:17:48.088
<v Tutor>Each molecule of trisodium citrate generates three molecules of bicarbonate and releases its calcium.

201
00:17:48.088 --> 00:17:54.091
<v Tutor>That is why the usual complication in a patient with a working liver is a metabolic alkalosis.

202
00:17:54.091 --> 00:17:59.565
<v Tutor>In liver failure, or in shock with poor tissue perfusion, citrate is not metabolised.

203
00:17:59.565 --> 00:18:00.919
<v Tutor>It accumulates.

204
00:18:00.919 --> 00:18:11.042
<v Tutor>The bound calcium stays bound, so the total calcium rises while the ionised calcium falls, and unmetabolised citrate is an unmeasured anion, so the anion gap opens.

205
00:18:11.042 --> 00:18:15.044
<v Tutor>The tell-tale sign is the ratio of total to ionised calcium.

206
00:18:15.044 --> 00:18:17.163
<v Tutor>Time for a pause and answer.

207
00:18:17.163 --> 00:18:22.990
<v Tutor>What ratio of total to ionised calcium suggests citrate accumulation, and why does it rise?

208
00:18:22.990 --> 00:18:24.520
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

209
00:18:29.920 --> 00:18:33.054
<v Tutor>A ratio above 2.5 suggests citrate accumulation.

210
00:18:33.054 --> 00:18:36.915
<v Tutor>In our patient it is 2.9 divided by 0.85, which is about 3.4.

211
00:18:36.915 --> 00:18:42.903
<v Tutor>It rises because the citrate-bound calcium is counted in the total but not in the ionised fraction.

212
00:18:42.903 --> 00:18:51.074
<v Tutor>The management is to reduce or stop the citrate, increase calcium replacement, and consider switching to heparin or to no anticoagulation.

213
00:18:51.074 --> 00:19:02.322
<v Tutor>A twist like this can earn you more than recall alone: KDIGO, in its 2012 acute kidney injury guideline, suggests regional citrate for continuous therapy in patients without a contraindication.

214
00:19:02.322 --> 00:19:06.239
<v Tutor>Severe liver failure is the classic relative contraindication.

215
00:19:06.239 --> 00:19:09.541
<v Tutor>And one last link back to part one of the question.

216
00:19:09.541 --> 00:19:15.417
<v Tutor>Continuous renal replacement therapy, run with phosphate-free fluids, steadily removes phosphate.

217
00:19:15.417 --> 00:19:22.916
<v Tutor>Hypophosphataemia on the filter is common and is often missed, and it is one reason patients fail to wean from the ventilator.

218
00:19:22.916 --> 00:19:28.400
<v Tutor>Check the phosphate daily and replace it, or use a phosphate-containing replacement fluid.

219
00:19:28.800 --> 00:19:30.042
<v Tutor>Revisit three.

220
00:19:30.042 --> 00:19:35.856
<v Tutor>A 77-year-old man is having a transurethral resection of the prostate under spinal anaesthesia.

221
00:19:35.856 --> 00:19:38.170
<v Tutor>He becomes restless and agitated.

222
00:19:38.170 --> 00:19:41.048
<v Tutor>He is intubated in theatre and sent to you.

223
00:19:41.048 --> 00:19:44.040
<v Tutor>This is the 2009 second sitting, question 21.

224
00:19:44.040 --> 00:19:48.217
<v Tutor>The biochemistry, read in order: sodium, 113 millimoles per litre.

225
00:19:48.217 --> 00:19:50.587
<v Tutor>Chloride, 87 millimoles per litre.

226
00:19:50.587 --> 00:19:53.071
<v Tutor>Potassium, 4.5 millimoles per litre.

227
00:19:53.071 --> 00:19:55.442
<v Tutor>Glucose, 5.1 millimoles per litre.

228
00:19:55.442 --> 00:19:57.643
<v Tutor>Urea, 5.0 millimoles per litre.

229
00:19:57.643 --> 00:20:00.973
<v Tutor>Measured osmolality, 280 milliosmoles per kilogram.

230
00:20:00.973 --> 00:20:13.447
<v Tutor>The parts ask you to describe the important biochemical abnormalities, the likely cause of the confusion, the transient neurological disturbance, two confirmatory tests, and whether hypertonic saline is indicated.

231
00:20:13.447 --> 00:20:18.414
<v Tutor>You scored three on this on the 14th of October and five on the 17th of October.

232
00:20:18.414 --> 00:20:21.124
<v Tutor>The diagnosis was right the second time.

233
00:20:21.124 --> 00:20:25.470
<v Tutor>The marks you left behind were in the calculation and the mechanisms.

234
00:20:25.470 --> 00:20:29.421
<v Tutor>So here is a pause and answer, and do the arithmetic properly.

235
00:20:29.421 --> 00:20:32.412
<v Tutor>Calculate the osmolar gap from these numbers.

236
00:20:32.412 --> 00:20:33.880
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

237
00:20:39.280 --> 00:20:44.200
<v Tutor>The calculated osmolality is two times the sodium, plus glucose, plus urea.

238
00:20:44.200 --> 00:20:48.467
<v Tutor>Two times 113 is 226, plus 5.1, plus 5.0, which gives about 236.

239
00:20:48.467 --> 00:20:50.482
<v Tutor>The measured value is 280.

240
00:20:50.482 --> 00:20:54.394
<v Tutor>The osmolar gap is therefore 44 milliosmoles per kilogram.

241
00:20:54.394 --> 00:21:00.499
<v Tutor>Anything over about 10 is abnormal, and a gap this large means an unmeasured osmole is present.

242
00:21:00.499 --> 00:21:08.738
<v Tutor>In this setting that osmole is glycine, absorbed from 1.5 percent glycine irrigating fluid through opened prostatic venous sinuses.

243
00:21:08.738 --> 00:21:13.124
<v Tutor>So this is severe hyponatraemia with a normal measured osmolality.

244
00:21:13.124 --> 00:21:15.554
<v Tutor>It is iso-osmolar, not hypotonic.

245
00:21:15.554 --> 00:21:18.814
<v Tutor>That one sentence answers most of the question.

246
00:21:18.814 --> 00:21:22.904
<v Tutor>The confusion is mostly glycine toxicity, not brain swelling.

247
00:21:22.904 --> 00:21:34.106
<v Tutor>Glycine is an inhibitory neurotransmitter in the retina, which explains the transient visual disturbance, sometimes complete blindness with fixed pupils, that resolves within hours.

248
00:21:34.106 --> 00:21:40.211
<v Tutor>It is also a co-agonist at the NMDA receptor, which contributes to encephalopathy and seizures.

249
00:21:40.211 --> 00:21:45.783
<v Tutor>And it is metabolised to serine and ammonia, so hyperammonaemia adds to the confusion.

250
00:21:45.783 --> 00:21:50.288
<v Tutor>The two confirmatory tests are serum glycine, and ammonia or serine.

251
00:21:50.288 --> 00:21:52.599
<v Tutor>Is hypertonic saline indicated?

252
00:21:52.599 --> 00:21:55.800
<v Tutor>No, because the measured osmolality is normal.

253
00:21:55.980 --> 00:22:00.079
<v Tutor>There is no osmotic gradient driving water into the brain.

254
00:22:00.079 --> 00:22:05.793
<v Tutor>The College answer said to consider it only if the measured osmolality is below 260.

255
00:22:05.793 --> 00:22:12.376
<v Tutor>The gas shows a pH of 7.33, a carbon dioxide of 33 millimetres of mercury and a bicarbonate of 16.

256
00:22:12.376 --> 00:22:20.077
<v Tutor>Using Winter's formula, the expected carbon dioxide is one and a half times 16, plus 8, which is 32, give or take 2.

257
00:22:20.077 --> 00:22:26.226
<v Tutor>So this is a normal anion gap metabolic acidosis with appropriate respiratory compensation.

258
00:22:26.226 --> 00:22:32.685
<v Tutor>The mechanism, in Stewart terms, is that the absorbed fluid has a strong ion difference of zero.

259
00:22:32.685 --> 00:22:39.579
<v Tutor>It lowers the extracellular strong ion difference more than it dilutes the weak acids, so the pH falls.

260
00:22:39.579 --> 00:22:46.286
<v Tutor>Seven hours later the sodium is 130, the urea has risen to 10.6, and the measured osmolality is 281.

261
00:22:46.286 --> 00:22:53.118
<v Tutor>The calculated value is now 2 times 130, plus 5.5, plus 10.6, about 276, so the gap has almost closed.

262
00:22:53.118 --> 00:22:58.459
<v Tutor>The glycine has been metabolised, and the rise in urea reflects its breakdown.

263
00:22:58.459 --> 00:23:04.732
<v Tutor>The registrar is worried that the sodium is correcting too fast, a rise of 17 in seven hours.

264
00:23:04.732 --> 00:23:09.700
<v Tutor>There is a basis to think about it, but the concern does not apply here.

265
00:23:09.880 --> 00:23:17.811
<v Tutor>The osmolality has not changed, so brain cells have not had to adapt to hypotonicity, and the risk of osmotic demyelination is low.

266
00:23:17.811 --> 00:23:24.486
<v Tutor>One more thing the examiners like: the differential of agitation during a resection under spinal anaesthesia.

267
00:23:24.486 --> 00:23:38.750
<v Tutor>Before you settle on TURP syndrome, consider hypoxaemia, a high spinal block, bladder perforation with pain from extravasated fluid, haemorrhage with hypotension, hypothermia from cold irrigating fluid, and bacteraemia from the urinary tract.

268
00:23:38.750 --> 00:23:43.200
<v Tutor>A sentence that rules these in or out shows judgment, not just recall.

269
00:23:44.100 --> 00:23:46.036
<v Examiner>Now I change one number.

270
00:23:46.036 --> 00:23:49.483
<v Examiner>Same patient, but the measured osmolality is 252.

271
00:23:49.483 --> 00:23:56.621
<v Examiner>The surgeon was using sterile water, or the resection went on for ninety minutes and he absorbed three litres.

272
00:23:56.621 --> 00:23:57.952
<v Examiner>He is seizing.

273
00:23:57.952 --> 00:23:58.980
<v Examiner>What now?

274
00:23:59.480 --> 00:24:05.086
<v Candidate>Then this is hypotonic hyponatraemia with cerebral oedema, and it is an emergency.

275
00:24:05.086 --> 00:24:16.858
<v Candidate>I would give 150 millilitres of 3 percent saline over 20 minutes, check the sodium, and repeat up to twice more, aiming for a rise of about 5 millimoles per litre in the first hour.

276
00:24:16.858 --> 00:24:21.218
<v Candidate>Then I would limit the total rise to 10 in the first 24 hours.

277
00:24:21.218 --> 00:24:25.080
<v Candidate>I would also treat the seizures with a benzodiazepine.

278
00:24:25.580 --> 00:24:29.105
<v Examiner>That matches the 2014 European guideline on hyponatraemia.

279
00:24:29.105 --> 00:24:31.615
<v Examiner>But you have assumed his volume status.

280
00:24:31.615 --> 00:24:33.591
<v Examiner>He has absorbed three litres.

281
00:24:33.591 --> 00:24:35.300
<v Examiner>What is his chest doing?

282
00:24:35.800 --> 00:24:42.318
<v Candidate>He may well have pulmonary oedema and hypertension from the fluid load, then hypotension as it redistributes.

283
00:24:42.318 --> 00:24:47.388
<v Candidate>So I would examine him and image the lungs, and give frusemide if he is overloaded.

284
00:24:47.388 --> 00:24:55.800
<v Candidate>If there is renal impairment and severe glycine or ammonia toxicity, haemodialysis can clear both, which links back to the dialysis discussion.

285
00:24:56.700 --> 00:24:57.459
<v Tutor>Good.

286
00:24:57.459 --> 00:25:02.827
<v Tutor>Notice the higher-level layer: the osmolality decides the treatment, not the sodium.

287
00:25:02.827 --> 00:25:05.044
<v Tutor>Two more points for the twist.

288
00:25:05.044 --> 00:25:12.338
<v Tutor>First, modern bipolar resection uses normal saline as the irrigant, so the classic glycine syndrome is becoming rare.

289
00:25:12.338 --> 00:25:20.508
<v Tutor>But saline absorption still causes volume overload and a hyperchloraemic acidosis, so the syndrome has changed rather than vanished.

290
00:25:20.508 --> 00:25:23.367
<v Tutor>Second, the College has not forgotten it.

291
00:25:23.367 --> 00:25:28.444
<v Tutor>TURP syndrome came back twice in the 2022 second sitting, as questions 6 and 7.

292
00:25:28.444 --> 00:25:29.669
<v Tutor>So expect it.

293
00:25:29.669 --> 00:25:37.780
<v Tutor>Your fix from both attempts is the same: write the osmolar gap calculation out in full, then give the reason behind each yes or no.

294
00:25:38.180 --> 00:25:41.623
<v Tutor>Revisit four, and this one is a gas-reading workout.

295
00:25:41.623 --> 00:25:47.132
<v Tutor>It is the 2013 second sitting, question 26, which is four short data questions in a row.

296
00:25:47.132 --> 00:25:50.747
<v Tutor>You scored one and a half on it on the 20th of October.

297
00:25:50.747 --> 00:25:58.207
<v Tutor>You answered only the first part, and you used the wrong compensation formula, so the other three parts never got started.

298
00:25:58.207 --> 00:26:02.969
<v Tutor>This is a speed problem as much as a knowledge problem, so let us drill it.

299
00:26:02.969 --> 00:26:03.945
<v Tutor>Part one.

300
00:26:03.945 --> 00:26:11.060
<v Tutor>A 46-year-old man has been ventilated for three weeks with severe pneumonia and acute respiratory distress syndrome.

301
00:26:11.060 --> 00:26:24.603
<v Tutor>On an inspired oxygen fraction of 0.6, the pH is 7.5, the arterial oxygen is 79 millimetres of mercury, the carbon dioxide is 45 millimetres of mercury, the bicarbonate is 36 millimoles per litre, and the base excess is plus 12.

302
00:26:24.603 --> 00:26:28.333
<v Tutor>The sodium is 138, the potassium 5.0 and the chloride 97.

303
00:26:28.333 --> 00:26:30.456
<v Tutor>Here is the pause and answer.

304
00:26:30.456 --> 00:26:36.768
<v Tutor>What is the expected carbon dioxide for this metabolic alkalosis, and is the compensation appropriate?

305
00:26:36.768 --> 00:26:38.260
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

306
00:26:43.660 --> 00:26:50.050
<v Tutor>The formula for metabolic alkalosis is 0.7 times the bicarbonate, plus 20, give or take 5.

307
00:26:50.050 --> 00:26:53.571
<v Tutor>So 0.7 times 36 is 25.2, plus 20, which is 45.

308
00:26:53.571 --> 00:26:56.310
<v Tutor>The measured carbon dioxide is 45.

309
00:26:56.310 --> 00:27:04.787
<v Tutor>So this is a metabolic alkalosis with appropriate respiratory compensation, and there is no separate respiratory disorder.

310
00:27:04.787 --> 00:27:14.242
<v Tutor>The formula you used, 0.8 times the carbon dioxide, takes the wrong variable, and it led you to call a second disorder that is not there.

311
00:27:14.242 --> 00:27:16.915
<v Tutor>Learn the four formulas as a set.

312
00:27:16.915 --> 00:27:23.370
<v Tutor>In metabolic acidosis, Winter's formula: 1.5 times the bicarbonate, plus 8, give or take 2.

313
00:27:23.370 --> 00:27:27.739
<v Tutor>In metabolic alkalosis: 0.7 times the bicarbonate, plus 20.

314
00:27:27.739 --> 00:27:36.477
<v Tutor>In respiratory acidosis, the bicarbonate rises by 1 for every 10 of carbon dioxide acutely, and by 4 for every 10 chronically.

315
00:27:36.477 --> 00:27:41.498
<v Tutor>In respiratory alkalosis, it falls by 2 acutely and by 5 chronically.

316
00:27:41.498 --> 00:27:45.149
<v Tutor>The College also gave marks for the oxygenation.

317
00:27:45.149 --> 00:27:56.365
<v Tutor>The alveolar gas equation gives an alveolar oxygen of about 371 millimetres of mercury at this inspired fraction, so the alveolar to arterial gradient is about 290.

318
00:27:56.365 --> 00:28:05.689
<v Tutor>The ratio of arterial oxygen to inspired oxygen is 79 divided by 0.6, about 130, which is moderate acute respiratory distress syndrome.

319
00:28:05.689 --> 00:28:07.580
<v Tutor>You had the gradient.

320
00:28:07.760 --> 00:28:19.800
<v Tutor>One likely cause: diuresis, or a post-hypercapnic alkalosis, where a chronic respiratory acidosis has been corrected by the ventilator but the kidneys have not yet excreted the retained bicarbonate.

321
00:28:20.200 --> 00:28:23.494
<v Tutor>Part two is the one that rewards a system.

322
00:28:23.494 --> 00:28:31.070
<v Tutor>A 75-year-old insulin-dependent woman with heart failure, on digoxin and a thiazide, arrives semi-comatose.

323
00:28:31.070 --> 00:28:40.490
<v Tutor>The pH is 7.40, the carbon dioxide 32, the bicarbonate 19, the potassium 2.7, the glucose 67 millimoles per litre and the anion gap 34.

324
00:28:40.490 --> 00:28:43.257
<v Tutor>A normal pH hides three disorders.

325
00:28:43.257 --> 00:28:50.635
<v Tutor>First, the anion gap of 34 means a severe high anion gap metabolic acidosis, from diabetic ketoacidosis.

326
00:28:50.635 --> 00:28:52.743
<v Tutor>Second, the delta ratio.

327
00:28:52.743 --> 00:28:56.695
<v Tutor>The anion gap has risen by 34 minus 12, which is 22.

328
00:28:56.695 --> 00:29:01.109
<v Tutor>The bicarbonate has fallen by only 24 minus 19, which is 5.

329
00:29:01.109 --> 00:29:04.074
<v Tutor>Twenty-two divided by 5 is about 4.4.

330
00:29:04.074 --> 00:29:15.207
<v Tutor>Anything above 2 means the bicarbonate is higher than it should be, so there is a coexisting metabolic alkalosis, here from the thiazide and potassium depletion.

331
00:29:15.207 --> 00:29:17.315
<v Tutor>Third, Winter's formula.

332
00:29:17.315 --> 00:29:23.573
<v Tutor>The expected carbon dioxide is 1.5 times 19, plus 8, which is about 36, give or take 2.

333
00:29:23.573 --> 00:29:29.568
<v Tutor>The measured value is 32, which is lower, so there is also a respiratory alkalosis.

334
00:29:29.568 --> 00:29:36.880
<v Tutor>That is a triple disorder, and a College answer of three lines scores well if each line has its number.

335
00:29:37.780 --> 00:29:39.468
<v Examiner>Now I make it harder.

336
00:29:39.468 --> 00:29:40.515
<v Examiner>Part four.

337
00:29:40.515 --> 00:29:46.858
<v Examiner>A 68-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes and a history of alcohol abuse presents with abdominal pain.

338
00:29:46.858 --> 00:29:53.550
<v Examiner>The pH is 6.87, the carbon dioxide is 8, the bicarbonate is 1.4 millimoles per litre and the lactate is 16.

339
00:29:53.550 --> 00:29:55.295
<v Examiner>Her creatinine is 170.

340
00:29:55.295 --> 00:29:56.925
<v Examiner>She is on metformin.

341
00:29:56.925 --> 00:30:02.220
<v Examiner>Give me the diagnoses, and tell me when you would call the renal team for dialysis.

342
00:30:02.720 --> 00:30:12.496
<v Candidate>The diagnoses are ischaemic bowel, metformin-associated lactic acidosis, and thiamine deficiency, with pancreatitis and cardiogenic shock as other possibilities.

343
00:30:12.496 --> 00:30:17.413
<v Candidate>I would want a CT of the abdomen, or a laparotomy, and a lipase and troponin.

344
00:30:17.413 --> 00:30:22.041
<v Candidate>For dialysis, metformin is small and water soluble and it is dialysable.

345
00:30:22.041 --> 00:30:29.040
<v Candidate>I would dialyse with a pH this low and a lactate this high, especially with kidney injury reducing its clearance.

346
00:30:29.540 --> 00:30:30.281
<v Examiner>Good.

347
00:30:30.281 --> 00:30:42.657
<v Examiner>The EXTRIP workgroup recommends extracorporeal treatment in metformin-associated lactic acidosis when the lactate is above 20, the pH is 7.0 or less, or there is shock or a failure of standard supportive care.

348
00:30:42.657 --> 00:30:44.824
<v Examiner>Her pH puts her over the line.

349
00:30:44.824 --> 00:30:52.695
<v Examiner>Intermittent haemodialysis is the preferred first modality, with continuous therapy as the alternative when that is not available.

350
00:30:52.695 --> 00:30:56.744
<v Examiner>Continue until the lactate is below 3 and the pH is above 7.35.

351
00:30:56.744 --> 00:30:58.740
<v Examiner>But what did you leave out?

352
00:30:59.240 --> 00:31:00.462
<v Candidate>The thiamine.

353
00:31:00.462 --> 00:31:03.896
<v Candidate>I should give it straight away, before any glucose.

354
00:31:03.896 --> 00:31:08.960
<v Candidate>It is cheap, and type B lactic acidosis from thiamine deficiency is reversible.

355
00:31:09.860 --> 00:31:11.631
<v Tutor>That is the right instinct.

356
00:31:11.631 --> 00:31:14.666
<v Tutor>The higher-level lesson from all four parts is pace.

357
00:31:14.666 --> 00:31:17.398
<v Tutor>Each part is worth about two and a half marks.

358
00:31:17.398 --> 00:31:20.838
<v Tutor>Two lines with a number in each will pass every one of them.

359
00:31:20.838 --> 00:31:25.189
<v Tutor>One beautiful paragraph on part one, with nothing else, scores one and a half.

360
00:31:25.189 --> 00:31:29.540
<v Tutor>Write two or three lines under every part label before you deepen any of them.

361
00:31:30.940 --> 00:31:32.872
<v Tutor>Now the syllabus round-up.

362
00:31:32.872 --> 00:31:40.146
<v Tutor>Six items, all beyond this week, chosen because they are among the most examined topics not yet covered on this podcast.

363
00:31:40.146 --> 00:31:42.305
<v Tutor>Each one is at overview level.

364
00:31:42.305 --> 00:31:46.908
<v Tutor>The aim is to hang a framework you will fill in properly in its own week.

365
00:31:46.908 --> 00:31:49.523
<v Tutor>First, mechanical ventilatory support.

366
00:31:49.523 --> 00:32:00.547
<v Tutor>It underlies more than fifty past College questions, more than any other item left on the list, usually as ventilator waveforms, a ventilation strategy for a named condition, or weaning.

367
00:32:00.547 --> 00:32:02.763
<v Tutor>The examiner wants five things.

368
00:32:02.763 --> 00:32:17.027
<v Tutor>One, lung-protective ventilation in acute respiratory distress syndrome: a tidal volume of about 6 millilitres per kilogram of predicted body weight, a plateau pressure of 30 centimetres of water or less, and a driving pressure under about 15.

369
00:32:17.027 --> 00:32:21.175
<v Tutor>The ARMA trial in 2000 established the low tidal volume strategy.

370
00:32:21.175 --> 00:32:27.256
<v Tutor>Two, PEEP chosen by a table or by response, with an awareness that recruitment manoeuvres can harm.

371
00:32:27.256 --> 00:32:35.780
<v Tutor>Three, prone positioning for at least 16 hours a day when the ratio of arterial oxygen to inspired oxygen is below 150 millimetres of mercury.

372
00:32:35.960 --> 00:32:44.197
<v Tutor>Four, the mechanics of the ventilator: the difference between peak and plateau pressure, which separates airway resistance from compliance.

373
00:32:44.197 --> 00:32:49.969
<v Tutor>And five, structured weaning, with daily spontaneous breathing trials linked to sedation holds.

374
00:32:49.969 --> 00:32:57.589
<v Tutor>The governing guideline to name is the 2023 ESICM guideline on acute respiratory distress syndrome, by Grasselli and colleagues.

375
00:32:57.589 --> 00:33:04.314
<v Tutor>A high-yield phrase: a high peak with a normal plateau means resistance, such as a blocked tube or bronchospasm.

376
00:33:04.314 --> 00:33:10.646
<v Tutor>A high peak with a high plateau means compliance, such as pneumothorax, oedema or abdominal hypertension.

377
00:33:10.646 --> 00:33:12.999
<v Tutor>Two more angles the College likes.

378
00:33:12.999 --> 00:33:16.081
<v Tutor>Non-invasive ventilation has its own questions.

379
00:33:16.081 --> 00:33:28.353
<v Tutor>The classic indication is a hypercapnic exacerbation of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease with a pH below 7.35 and a carbon dioxide above 45 millimetres of mercury, where it reduces intubation and mortality.

380
00:33:28.353 --> 00:33:37.038
<v Tutor>The examiner also wants the contraindications and the markers of failure, such as no improvement in pH or respiratory rate within one to two hours.

381
00:33:37.038 --> 00:33:40.400
<v Tutor>And weaning failure is examined as a list of causes.

382
00:33:40.580 --> 00:33:54.675
<v Tutor>Use a framework of load against capacity: respiratory load, cardiac load including weaning-induced pulmonary oedema, neuromuscular weakness, neurological drive, and metabolic causes such as hypophosphataemia, which links straight back to revisit two.

383
00:33:54.675 --> 00:33:58.007
<v Tutor>Driving pressure is worth one sentence of physiology.

384
00:33:58.007 --> 00:34:03.634
<v Tutor>It is the plateau pressure minus the PEEP, which is the tidal volume divided by the compliance.

385
00:34:03.634 --> 00:34:08.660
<v Tutor>So it scales the breath to the size of the functional lung, the so-called baby lung.

386
00:34:09.060 --> 00:34:18.454
<v Tutor>Second, airway management, which underlies about 45 past questions, often as the failed or difficult intubation in ICU, or the patient with a threatened airway.

387
00:34:18.454 --> 00:34:26.002
<v Tutor>Intubation in ICU is more dangerous than in theatre, because the patient brings hypoxaemia, hypotension and acidosis with them.

388
00:34:26.002 --> 00:34:28.798
<v Tutor>The examiner wants preparation and a plan.

389
00:34:28.798 --> 00:34:30.140
<v Tutor>Use a checklist.

390
00:34:30.140 --> 00:34:35.732
<v Tutor>Preoxygenate with non-invasive ventilation or high-flow nasal oxygen in hypoxaemic patients.

391
00:34:35.732 --> 00:34:40.876
<v Tutor>Optimise the haemodynamics first, with fluid if appropriate and a vasopressor ready.

392
00:34:40.876 --> 00:34:43.783
<v Tutor>Use a videolaryngoscope as the first device.

393
00:34:43.783 --> 00:34:47.362
<v Tutor>Confirm placement with waveform capnography, every time.

394
00:34:47.362 --> 00:35:01.117
<v Tutor>The guideline to name is the 2018 Difficult Airway Society guideline for tracheal intubation in critically ill adults, written with the Intensive Care Society, the Faculty of Intensive Care Medicine and the Royal College of Anaesthetists.

395
00:35:01.117 --> 00:35:12.524
<v Tutor>Its plan is a maximum of three laryngoscopy attempts plus one by an expert, then a second-generation supraglottic airway, then face mask, then front-of-neck access with a scalpel, bougie and tube.

396
00:35:12.524 --> 00:35:15.823
<v Tutor>In Australia you can also name the Vortex approach.

397
00:35:15.823 --> 00:35:17.780
<v Tutor>Here is a pause and answer.

398
00:35:17.960 --> 00:35:22.018
<v Tutor>Name the three physiological killers at intubation in the critically ill.

399
00:35:22.018 --> 00:35:23.320
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

400
00:35:28.720 --> 00:35:31.773
<v Tutor>Hypoxaemia, hypotension and metabolic acidosis.

401
00:35:31.773 --> 00:35:41.044
<v Tutor>The acidotic patient who is compensating by breathing hard will acidify quickly once you stop that breathing with apnoea and a ventilator set to normal values.

402
00:35:41.044 --> 00:35:44.985
<v Tutor>So match the minute ventilation to their pre-intubation effort.

403
00:35:44.985 --> 00:35:47.372
<v Tutor>Two further points that earn marks.

404
00:35:47.372 --> 00:35:50.314
<v Tutor>First, post-intubation hypotension is common.

405
00:35:50.314 --> 00:36:01.861
<v Tutor>It comes from induction agents, loss of sympathetic drive and positive pressure reducing venous return, so have a vasopressor running or drawn up, and choose a haemodynamically gentler induction dose.

406
00:36:01.861 --> 00:36:05.803
<v Tutor>Second, extubating a known difficult airway needs its own plan.

407
00:36:05.803 --> 00:36:14.851
<v Tutor>That means an awake extubation in a controlled setting, with an airway exchange catheter considered, and the equipment and people present for reintubation.

408
00:36:14.851 --> 00:36:20.847
<v Tutor>Third, thrombotic disease, which in practice means pulmonary embolism, with about 39 past questions.

409
00:36:20.847 --> 00:36:29.007
<v Tutor>There is a new guideline this year, the 2026 multisociety guideline from the American Heart Association and American College of Cardiology.

410
00:36:29.007 --> 00:36:34.947
<v Tutor>It replaces the old low, intermediate and high risk language with five clinical categories, A to E.

411
00:36:34.947 --> 00:36:38.000
<v Tutor>Category A is an incidental, asymptomatic clot.

412
00:36:38.180 --> 00:36:43.160
<v Tutor>B is symptomatic with low severity, for example a simplified severity index of zero.

413
00:36:43.160 --> 00:36:45.758
<v Tutor>C is symptomatic with elevated severity.

414
00:36:45.758 --> 00:36:53.823
<v Tutor>D is incipient cardiopulmonary failure, meaning transient hypotension or normotensive shock, such as a raised lactate or acute kidney injury.

415
00:36:53.823 --> 00:37:00.751
<v Tutor>E is persistent hypotension or cardiopulmonary failure, with refractory shock or cardiac arrest as its most severe form.

416
00:37:00.751 --> 00:37:11.360
<v Tutor>For category E, systemic thrombolysis, catheter-directed thrombolysis and mechanical thrombectomy are all given the same reasonable-to-use rating, so systemic lysis no longer has priority.

417
00:37:11.360 --> 00:37:20.832
<v Tutor>Low molecular weight heparin is preferred over unfractionated heparin in hospitalised patients, and a pulmonary embolism response team should assess categories C to E.

418
00:37:20.832 --> 00:37:26.028
<v Tutor>The College will still accept the European Society of Cardiology language, so know both.

419
00:37:26.028 --> 00:37:29.438
<v Tutor>And know the physiology of the failing right ventricle.

420
00:37:29.438 --> 00:37:40.101
<v Tutor>Avoid large fluid boluses, keep the systemic pressure up with noradrenaline to protect right coronary perfusion, and be very wary of induction and positive pressure, which can cause arrest.

421
00:37:40.101 --> 00:37:45.460
<v Tutor>For systemic thrombolysis, the standard alteplase regimen is 100 milligrams over two hours.

422
00:37:45.640 --> 00:37:55.053
<v Tutor>In peri-arrest or cardiac arrest from presumed pulmonary embolism, a bolus is given, and resuscitation should continue for long enough for the drug to work.

423
00:37:55.053 --> 00:38:05.155
<v Tutor>Know the absolute contraindications: previous haemorrhagic stroke, recent ischaemic stroke, active bleeding, recent major surgery or trauma, and an intracranial tumour.

424
00:38:05.155 --> 00:38:16.520
<v Tutor>Where lysis is contraindicated or has failed, catheter-directed therapy, surgical embolectomy, or venoarterial ECMO as a bridge are the options a pulmonary embolism response team will weigh.

425
00:38:16.920 --> 00:38:21.388
<v Tutor>Fourth, poisoning and drug intoxication, with about 39 past questions.

426
00:38:21.388 --> 00:38:35.480
<v Tutor>The examiner rewards a structure, and the Australian one is the sequence taught in the Toxicology Handbook: resuscitation, risk assessment, supportive care, investigations, decontamination, enhanced elimination, antidotes and disposition.

427
00:38:35.480 --> 00:38:38.516
<v Tutor>Risk assessment is the step that earns marks.

428
00:38:38.516 --> 00:38:47.738
<v Tutor>It means the agent, the dose in milligrams per kilogram, the time since ingestion, the clinical features and patient factors, and it predicts the course.

429
00:38:47.738 --> 00:38:53.008
<v Tutor>The screening tests in every overdose are an ECG, a paracetamol level and a glucose.

430
00:38:53.008 --> 00:38:58.737
<v Tutor>For paracetamol, name the 2019 Australian and New Zealand guideline by Chiew and colleagues.

431
00:38:58.737 --> 00:39:11.568
<v Tutor>It uses a single nomogram line starting at 150 milligrams per litre at four hours, and a two-bag acetylcysteine regimen, 200 milligrams per kilogram over four hours and then 100 milligrams per kilogram over 16 hours.

432
00:39:11.568 --> 00:39:18.499
<v Tutor>The dose in the second bag is increased for large ingestions with a concentration above double the nomogram line.

433
00:39:18.499 --> 00:39:22.280
<v Tutor>For enhanced elimination, know which drugs are dialysable.

434
00:39:22.460 --> 00:39:32.670
<v Tutor>These are small, water soluble, with a small volume of distribution and low protein binding, such as lithium, toxic alcohols, salicylate, metformin and valproate.

435
00:39:32.670 --> 00:39:42.100
<v Tutor>The EXTRIP workgroup publishes recommendations on when to dialyse for specific poisons, and we have just used its metformin criteria in revisit four.

436
00:39:42.100 --> 00:39:47.566
<v Tutor>Finally, know a short list of antidotes with doses, because examiners ask for them.

437
00:39:47.566 --> 00:39:57.716
<v Tutor>Sodium bicarbonate for tricyclic cardiotoxicity with a QRS longer than 100 milliseconds, 1 to 2 millimoles per kilogram as a bolus, aiming for a pH of about 7.5.

438
00:39:57.716 --> 00:40:07.386
<v Tutor>Digoxin-specific antibody fragments for digoxin toxicity with life-threatening arrhythmia or a potassium above 5 millimoles per litre in acute poisoning.

439
00:40:07.386 --> 00:40:11.951
<v Tutor>High-dose insulin euglycaemia for calcium channel blocker poisoning.

440
00:40:11.951 --> 00:40:20.900
<v Tutor>And naloxone in small titrated doses for opioid toxicity, so you reverse the respiratory depression without precipitating violent withdrawal.

441
00:40:21.300 --> 00:40:32.938
<v Tutor>Fifth, haemostatic failure, with about 37 past questions, spanning trauma coagulopathy, disseminated intravascular coagulation, heparin-induced thrombocytopenia and anticoagulant reversal.

442
00:40:32.938 --> 00:40:42.973
<v Tutor>For bleeding trauma, name the European guideline on management of major bleeding and coagulopathy following trauma, sixth edition, Rossaint and colleagues, 2023.

443
00:40:42.973 --> 00:40:45.229
<v Tutor>The key points are as follows.

444
00:40:45.229 --> 00:40:53.364
<v Tutor>Give tranexamic acid within three hours of injury, 1 gram over 10 minutes and then 1 gram over 8 hours, the dose used in CRASH-2.

445
00:40:53.364 --> 00:41:05.477
<v Tutor>Use permissive hypotension, a systolic pressure of 80 to 90 millimetres of mercury until bleeding is controlled, but not in traumatic brain injury, where a mean pressure of at least 80 is advised.

446
00:41:05.477 --> 00:41:12.662
<v Tutor>Replace fibrinogen when it is below 1.5 grams per litre, or when viscoelastic testing shows a functional deficit.

447
00:41:12.662 --> 00:41:15.809
<v Tutor>Keep the ionised calcium in the normal range.

448
00:41:15.809 --> 00:41:30.060
<v Tutor>For disseminated intravascular coagulation, the International Society on Thrombosis and Haemostasis score uses platelets, a fibrin marker such as D-dimer, prothrombin time and fibrinogen, and a score of five or more means overt DIC.

449
00:41:30.240 --> 00:41:37.365
<v Tutor>For heparin-induced thrombocytopenia, use the 4Ts score: thrombocytopenia, timing, thrombosis and other causes.

450
00:41:37.365 --> 00:41:43.473
<v Tutor>Then stop all heparin and start a non-heparin anticoagulant such as argatroban or bivalirudin.

451
00:41:43.473 --> 00:41:45.688
<v Tutor>Note the link to revisit two.

452
00:41:45.688 --> 00:41:52.634
<v Tutor>In a patient on the filter with this condition, citrate becomes the anticoagulant of choice for the circuit.

453
00:41:52.634 --> 00:41:56.226
<v Tutor>Anticoagulant reversal is the other common question.

454
00:41:56.226 --> 00:42:09.399
<v Tutor>For warfarin with major bleeding, give a prothrombin complex concentrate, in Australia Prothrombinex, at about 25 to 50 international units per kilogram, together with 5 to 10 milligrams of intravenous vitamin K.

455
00:42:09.399 --> 00:42:12.393
<v Tutor>For dabigatran, give idarucizumab 5 grams.

456
00:42:12.393 --> 00:42:20.297
<v Tutor>For the factor ten inhibitors, apixaban and rivaroxaban, a prothrombin complex concentrate is the usual Australian approach.

457
00:42:20.297 --> 00:42:28.320
<v Tutor>In every case, add the general measures: local haemostasis, temperature above 35 degrees, calcium, and correction of acidosis.

458
00:42:28.720 --> 00:42:35.355
<v Tutor>Sixth, sepsis and septic shock, with about 31 past questions, and the link back to this week's shock block.

459
00:42:35.355 --> 00:42:38.009
<v Tutor>There is also a new guideline to know.

460
00:42:38.009 --> 00:42:46.259
<v Tutor>The Surviving Sepsis Campaign 2026 adult guidelines were published in March 2026 in Critical Care Medicine and Intensive Care Medicine.

461
00:42:46.259 --> 00:42:49.259
<v Tutor>They contain 129 statements, 46 of them new.

462
00:42:49.259 --> 00:42:52.374
<v Tutor>The points that matter for the exam are these.

463
00:42:52.374 --> 00:42:57.163
<v Tutor>For septic shock, give antimicrobials immediately, ideally within one hour.

464
00:42:57.163 --> 00:43:02.471
<v Tutor>For sepsis without shock, give them early but leave room for diagnostic stewardship.

465
00:43:02.471 --> 00:43:06.452
<v Tutor>After a loading dose, use extended infusions of beta-lactams.

466
00:43:06.452 --> 00:43:09.394
<v Tutor>De-escalate once the microbiology is known.

467
00:43:09.394 --> 00:43:12.740
<v Tutor>Use balanced crystalloids in preference to saline.

468
00:43:12.740 --> 00:43:22.317
<v Tutor>Target a mean arterial pressure of about 65 millimetres of mercury, managed as a range, with a lower range of 60 to 65 suggested for patients aged 65 or over.

469
00:43:22.317 --> 00:43:26.067
<v Tutor>And remove fluid actively once resuscitation is complete.

470
00:43:26.067 --> 00:43:36.279
<v Tutor>The 2026 guideline is new, so for its exact wording on vasopressin and corticosteroids, read the document itself before the exam rather than relying on older thresholds.

471
00:43:36.279 --> 00:43:38.760
<v Tutor>The definitions are still Sepsis-3.

472
00:43:38.940 --> 00:43:46.975
<v Tutor>Sepsis is life-threatening organ dysfunction from a dysregulated host response to infection, marked by a rise in SOFA score of two or more.

473
00:43:46.975 --> 00:43:55.392
<v Tutor>Septic shock means a vasopressor is needed to keep the mean pressure at 65 and the lactate is above 2 millimoles per litre despite adequate fluid.

474
00:43:55.392 --> 00:44:00.421
<v Tutor>When sepsis comes up, examiners reward source control named specifically, and timed.

475
00:44:00.421 --> 00:44:04.848
<v Tutor>Say what the source is, how it will be controlled, by whom, and how soon.

476
00:44:04.848 --> 00:44:08.073
<v Tutor>Re-measure a raised lactate to guide resuscitation.

477
00:44:08.073 --> 00:44:16.053
<v Tutor>You can mention capillary refill time as an alternative target, which ANDROMEDA-SHOCK supported and which we covered in an earlier spiral.

478
00:44:16.053 --> 00:44:22.448
<v Tutor>And when asked about fluids, describe the four phases: rescue, optimisation, stabilisation and de-escalation.

479
00:44:22.448 --> 00:44:28.351
<v Tutor>The 2026 recommendation on active fluid removal is the de-escalation phase written into a guideline.

480
00:44:28.351 --> 00:44:30.701
<v Tutor>Here is the final pause and answer.

481
00:44:30.701 --> 00:44:33.871
<v Tutor>A 70-year-old in septic shock is on noradrenaline.

482
00:44:33.871 --> 00:44:42.999
<v Tutor>What mean arterial pressure range does the 2026 guideline suggest as the initial target for her, and what other fluid strategy does it add after resuscitation?

483
00:44:42.999 --> 00:44:44.420
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

484
00:44:49.820 --> 00:44:54.030
<v Tutor>A range of 60 to 65 millimetres of mercury, because she is 65 or older.

485
00:44:54.030 --> 00:44:58.719
<v Tutor>And after resuscitation, it suggests actively removing fluid to reduce overload.

486
00:44:58.719 --> 00:45:05.220
<v Tutor>That ties neatly back to the cardiorenal patient in revisit one, where fluid made things worse rather than better.

487
00:45:05.620 --> 00:45:07.184
<v Tutor>That is today's spiral.

488
00:45:07.184 --> 00:45:09.605
<v Tutor>One thing to do before the next episode.

489
00:45:09.605 --> 00:45:15.860
<v Tutor>In today's set, write the osmolar gap and Winter's formula out in full when you revisit the 2018 acid-base question.

490
00:45:15.860 --> 00:45:21.913
<v Tutor>Then, for the renal replacement question, write one line that defines convection before you write anything else.

491
00:45:21.913 --> 00:45:27.865
<v Tutor>Under every part label, write two or three lines before you deepen any of them, and move on at twelve minutes.

492
00:45:27.865 --> 00:45:33.363
<v Tutor>And for the haemodynamic question on the cardiac surgical patient, use the same habit as revisit one.

493
00:45:33.363 --> 00:45:41.989
<v Tutor>Name the problems from the numbers first, the low cardiac index, the high pulmonary pressures and the hypothermia, then manage each in order of what kills soonest.

494
00:45:41.989 --> 00:45:43.300
<v Tutor>See you next time.
